.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt ASDO 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm asdo
.Nd Privilege escalation utility
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl e
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl u Ar user
.Op Fl a Ar user Ns / Ns Ar group
.Ar command Op Ar args
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
can leverage
.Xr mdo 1
on
.Fx 15.0
or later to escalate privileges with proper authentication.
.Xr mac_do 4
manages authorization while
.Xr su 1
manages authentication.
.Pp
To use
.Nm
add a new user with its username ending in
.Qq _do
and set a secure password for it.
Then write the desired
.Xr mac_do 4
rules for the new user's uid.
Test that the rules work as expected and make them permanent by writing them to
.Pa /etc/sysctl.conf .
.Sh OPTIONS
The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl e
This option keeps the environment variables and working directory intact.
.It Fl s
This option simulates a fresh login, discarding both
the environment variables and working directory.
.It Fl u Ar user
Specifies the user to run the command as, defaults to root.
.It Fl a Ar user Ns / Ns Ar group
Specifies the user to authenticate against (without the
.Qq _do
suffix), defaults to your username and
the name of your groups as a fallback.
.El
.Pp
The
.Fl e
and
.Fl s
options are mutually exclusive, the last one specified takes precedence.
.Pp
Default behaviour discards environment variables and creates
a minimal set, while keeping the working directory intact.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 1 on any errors, 0 otherwise.
.Pp
Due to architectural limitations,
.Nm
cannot determine the exact exit status of the executed command
and only detects if it is zero or non-zero.
A non-zero exit status is considered an error.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mdo 1 ,
.Xr su 1 ,
.Xr mac_do 4 ,
.Xr sysctl.conf 5
